A||||17||Alex4mula||HELP! Removing rack & pinion...||||11||
Z||000000||Alex4mula||02-07-2001||09:21 PM||||<BR>Trying to fix my 1/4" steering wheel play I tried to reach the screw mentioned in another thread with no luck. I tried to do it in an old R&P I have outside & could not take out the screw.<BR>Anyway, I started removing my R&P and I'm stuck. Everything is loose, but how the hell you take it out? From which side? It seems there's no room. I did it once about 8 years ago but I don't remember! Do I need to take out that top big nut for clearance? That's the one that seems to be htting the frame. Please help!!||||reg||11
Z||000001||N_Man||02-07-2001||10:09 PM||||alex i took out my rack last week.<BR>steps are <BR>1 take off tires<BR>2 press out outer tie rod ends from the hub assmbly<BR>3 undo the 4 mounting bolts<BR>4 compleatly remove the pinch bolt<BR>5 get the rack free from the steering column<BR>6 and finally you should be able to tilt it and pull it out from the side of the car<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Alex4mula||02-07-2001||10:21 PM||||Did steps 1-5. Step 6 is were the problem is. From which side it comes out? Driver or passenger? The top big nut on top of the steering column shaft is the one I have clearance problems from the drivers side. Do I need to take it off? I think that's what I did before but I'm not sure. My car is an 88 Formula if that matters.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Bob Englert||02-07-2001||10:23 PM||||You might need to pry apart the u shaped clamp the pinch bolt goes through in order to pull the rack free of the steering shaft.<P>The 4 rack mounting bolts are very easy to break when trying to remove them.  I would recommend using a lot of PB Blaster before you loosen them.  If you break one just use a dremel tool with a cutoff wheel and cut out a s much of the welded nut as possible.  You can then use a chisel and break the nut free of the frame.  Then use a high strength nut and bolt when reassembling.<P>I have found it easier to remove the front compartment pan which makes it very easy to get to the upper bolts, particularly if one or more break off.<P><BR>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl<p>[This message has been edited by Bob Englert (edited 02-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Alex4mula||02-07-2001||10:34 PM||||Thanks Bob. I didn't have problems with the bolts. The R&P is completely loose. I did remove the clamps. What I can't figure out to do is how to remove the R&P out of the chassis. Is there's any trick? Is it like a puzzle?||||reg||1
Z||000005||sluppy123||02-07-2001||10:57 PM||||Did you remove the two cross member braces. Each is held on by two bolts, they run from front to back. Remove these and it will drop right out. (I know this works on 84-87's I don't know if 88's have the braces or not?)<P>------------------<BR>-Brian, Toolmaker<BR>Lincoln, NE<BR>85' SC & 87' GT-Leather, Lowered and On The Bottle<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=247832&a=7653930&p=37756786&Sequence=0"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||N_Man||02-08-2001||12:11 AM||||Alex<BR>i dont know if the 88 makes a big difference<BR>but is your rack input compleatly free of the clamp... it sounds like the part your having problems with is the rack input. right?<BR>ummm i pulled mine out from the drivers side but im not sure it would make a difference.  But I did have to tilt the rack so the input shaft would clear the cross member.<BR>I hate taking off more stuff then i have to to do a job so i left my braces on, but if that will solve your problem i dont think it would be much work...<P>good luck||||reg||1
Z||000007||Alex4mula||02-08-2001||08:36 AM||||N_Man, Rack is free of everything.<BR>Slupy, I think you can't remove cross member braces in 88s.<BR>Any other hint??  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||Rodney||02-08-2001||09:01 AM||||Did you disconnect the steering shaft? I removed the rack from my 88 and it was easy. Pull the front trunk out. Only takes a few minutes.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Bob Englert||02-08-2001||09:41 AM||||There shouldn't be any reason you can't remove the crossmember braces on the 88.  It has been a while since I had my Formula, but I don't remember any specific problems removing the rack with the crossmember braces out.  <P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000010||Alex4mula||02-08-2001||10:20 AM||||I whish I could have a photo. Picture worth a 1000 words. How you disconnect the steering shaft? Is that the shaft that protudes from the R&P and that connects to the steering column mechanism? Like 6" long? That would make it but I didn't know you could take that out. Is it press in?<BR>Which cross member braces are we talking about? Are those the "C" shaped braces that hold the rack to the chassis? If its those then yes I took them off.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Voytek||02-08-2001||02:20 PM||||After you removed the pinch screw (by the gas/brake pedal) you should be able to slide out the main steering shaft (connected to the steering column) from the shaft connected to the rack.  I've done this before and had hell of a time doing it - they tend to seize with time.  N man is right - you need to remove screw completely.  It makes it easier to separate the two if you hammer in a flat screw driver between where the pinch screw is normally located. (if this was your question)||||reg||1
Z||000012||Alex4mula||02-08-2001||03:32 PM||||From Voytek description my steering shaft is disconnected. The whole R&P is not attached to anything. Is just that it doesn't fit thru the small cavity in the chassis by a 1/4 inch. Unless I take out the preload nut (big nut on top that looks like a cap). Do I have to do that??? By this time you should think I'm dumb & don't understand this... Sorry if that's the case...||||reg||1
Z||000013||Phil||02-08-2001||04:50 PM||||Don't feel dumb or stupid - took me forever to get a totally detached alt. out of an 88GT||||reg||1
Z||000014||Alex4mula||02-08-2001||05:57 PM||||Well I give up. Was able to adjust preload with R&P hanging. At least I felt the play while adjusting & I think it improved a little. Lets see once I put it back. Thanks all for your help.||||reg||1
Z||000015||DRH||02-08-2001||06:18 PM||||Alex,<P>Just to let you know you're not alone. I remember having one heck of a time getting the rack out of my Formula a couple of years ago (an hour or more AFTER everything was loose). It did finally come out with the right combination of twisting and turning. I couldn't begin to describe how I finally did it but it didn't involve removing parts from the rack or extraordinary parts from the car. Also, it seems it came out (and went back in) fairly easily once I figured out the trick.<P>David.||||reg||1
Z||000016||oneof547||02-08-2001||11:26 PM||||Alex<P>I tried e-mailing u directly but no luck.  Maybe a second set of eyes and hands that r used to working on 88's would help call me<P>my # is 722-8388 same area code as u, dial the # direct, I can drop by and maybe help u out.<P>Laters<BR>oneof547                    88 formula t-tops||||reg||7
Z||000017||Alex4mula||02-09-2001||09:04 AM||||Thanks a lot 547. Well, finished adjusting it and mounted everything back already. Test drive this morning shows BIIIG improvement  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. Still need the final test in the groves of I95. Will do at lunch. Thanks ALL for your help and for guding me to that simple fix. I was ready to pour $200+ in a new R&P  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
